WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.746
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.746 --> 00:00:03.510
- [Adam Falk] At LinkedIn's
flagship office space,

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.810
there are more than 75
different types of seating.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.510
- This feels like a spot for
maybe a really quick call

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.330
or just answering a couple of emails.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.090
Work or NBA Jam.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.376
Yeah. This is your
space. And this is mine.

00:00:17.376 --> 00:00:18.630
- Yeah. This is my space,
and that's your space.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.560
- [Adam Falk] Many of these seats

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.570
were actually meant to be desks.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.060
- So I think during our initial plans,

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.180
we had just standard desks in this space

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.550
with traditional conference
rooms and huddle rooms.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.680
- [Adam Falk] But the
pandemic changed that.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.450
- How much of what we're
seeing in this space

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.220
is new as a result of the pandemic?

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.330
- 100% of it.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.620
- [Adam Falk] This recently opened space

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.390
is designed for hybrid work,

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.550
a model that has emerged
as the leading choice

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:42.960
for LinkedIn and others,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.990
with 42% of people with
remote capable jobs

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.400
working partly at home,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.800
according to a February Gallup poll.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.320
So what does a hybrid office look like?

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.320
I toured Building One with the
project's leaders to find out

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.990
and to get a glimpse into
what could be the future

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.193
of workplace design.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:01.813
(upbeat music)

00:01:01.813 --> 00:01:04.020
Building One is the new
hub of LinkedIn's campus

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.190
in Silicon valley.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.090
It has six floors, roughly
239,000 square feet,

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.760
and room for about 1,500 employees

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.370
- Prior to the pandemic, when
we looked at this building,

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.960
really at that point the main goal

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.500
was fitting as many people
as we could in this space.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.670
- [Adam Falk] The original floor plans

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.790
called for 1,080 individual workstations.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.850
The thinking was, "One
employee, one desk."

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.610
- Once we were sent home
during the pandemic,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.110
a lot of that changed.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.120
And much of what we
were trying to solve for

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.450
before the pandemic was not the same thing

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.744
that we were looking at at that moment.

00:01:38.744 --> 00:01:39.577
(upbeat music)

00:01:39.577 --> 00:01:40.472
- [Adam Falk] So with the help

00:01:40.472 --> 00:01:41.305
of its design partners at NBBJ,

00:01:41.305 --> 00:01:43.140
LinkedIn retooled the space,

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.370
cutting the number of desks
in half to 569 workstations

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:50.760
and adding dozens of new,
nontraditional seating setups.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.920
- What did you and LinkedIn
learn from the pandemic

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.960
that would result in the
space that we're in right now?

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.900
- I think we really wanted to
do a lot of experimentation,

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.490
because we just didn't know.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.600
No one had a crystal ball

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.430
about what the future would actually be.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:03.360
So we wanted to provide

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.221
as many variety of spaces as possible.

00:02:06.221 --> 00:02:07.054
(upbeat music)

00:02:07.054 --> 00:02:07.887
(door opens)

00:02:07.887 --> 00:02:08.720
- [Adam Falk] The variety starts

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.830
right when employees walk in.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.640
The design does away with a
large lobby in favor of a cafe.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.100
- Daniel and Adam.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.230
- This is one of my favorite
spaces of the buildings.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.440
The idea of this space is
that the minute you walk in,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.690
you feel the buzz, there's
people getting coffee,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.670
there's people moving
throughout the space.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.190
- [Adam Falk] The cafe spills
into the first of two areas

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.500
LinkedIn calls its co-working spaces.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.630
Think open, non-reserved seating.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.670
The co-working area on the second floor

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.170
was added in the redesign.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.420
- So we've got primary work points here.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.680
- [Adam Falk] Desks with monitors

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.800
and tall backed booths with
Ottomans dot the space that,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.900
looking back at the original design,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.020
was once full of assigned workstations.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.510
- So a space like this is
great for those smaller

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.020
time periods where you might
come in and work with your team

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.330
and then go home and do some focus time.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:58.163
Or for vice versa.

00:02:58.163 --> 00:03:00.019
- And that's okay with LinkedIn,

00:03:00.019 --> 00:03:01.980
to come in for half the day

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.600
and work at home for half the day?

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.433
- Yeah

00:03:04.433 --> 00:03:07.987
LinkedIn's approach is that
we are leading with trust.

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:09.000
(upbeat music)

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.700
The idea is that we trust
our employees to make

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.090
the decision that's gonna be
best for them and their team.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.340
- [Adam Falk] Co-working is
open to anyone in the company

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.720
for any amount of time.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.160
Employees assigned to the building

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.730
who plan to work there all
day will likely move upstairs.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.480
- The concept behind how
the floors are planned is

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.360
the entry to the building
is the most social.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.400
And the farther that
you get into the space,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.720
it becomes more focused

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.940
- [Adam Falk] Workers sit
in so-called neighborhoods

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.070
or areas assigned to their teams.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.480
- We call this the living room.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.890
- [Adam Falk] Neighborhoods are split,

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.810
about half alternative workspaces

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.730
and half traditional desks.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.790
Those desks are mostly here in the back.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.070
- Was this sort of what the whole floor

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.960
was meant to look like
before the pandemic.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.000
- It is what the whole floor looked like

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.050
before the pandemic.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.200
- What led the decision
to get rid of desk space?

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.390
- Because we imagined that not everyone

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:02.580
is going to come to
work eight hours a day.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.140
- [Adam Falk] So rather
than fill the space

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.910
with potentially empty desks,

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:09.000
of which I still saw plenty,
new furniture was added

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.580
based on something called
the postures matrix.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.480
- The postures matrix is
about the amount of time

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.400
that we imagine a person would be working

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.810
doing a certain activity,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.600
combined with the amount of
ergonomics to support that.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.330
- So how long am I meant to
spend at this chair here?

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.330
- So as a high table we imagine
that you spend less time

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.610
compared to a low, standard table.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.090
So this would be used
maybe 30 to 60 minutes.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.740
- [Adam Falk] LinkedIn
employees in Building One

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.330
don't have assigned desks.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.970
Instead they can grab a seat
anywhere in their neighborhood.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.280
But it's not quite hot-desking either,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.710
as seats don't need to be reserved.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:44.940
- Even before the pandemic,

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.055
our desk utilization I
think was around 30 to 40%

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:50.010
- 30 to 40%?

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.890
- Meaning that even though people might be

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.230
in the office all day,
they're at meetings,

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.420
they're at lunch, they might be sick,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.920
or just not in the office.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.600
And so now we're really looking at

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.660
what does that desk utilization
look like in a hybrid world?

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.061
- Do you know what that is yet?

00:05:05.061 --> 00:05:05.894
- No.

00:05:05.894 --> 00:05:06.727
(laughter)

00:05:06.727 --> 00:05:07.560
- [Adam Falk] But in a hybrid office,

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.393
(upbeat music)

00:05:08.393 --> 00:05:11.400
the individual setup is arguably
easier to design for it.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.040
It's the conference room that's tricky.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.560
Here, LinkedIn is, again, experimenting,

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.960
from conference rooms that
look like living rooms-

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.240
- What we wanted to do was
to take away the formality

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.220
of meetings from offsite to onsite,

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.470
and put people in a neutral position

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.260
where if you are at home as
a part of the conversation,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.400
you're feeling very comfortable

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.690
interacting with your colleagues.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.700
- [Adam Falk] To conference
rooms packed with tech,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.950
including cameras
positioned at table height

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.930
that reframe to better show the speaker.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:38.730
- So it makes it a little bit more

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.440
like you're sitting at the table.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.273
- [Adam Falk] And cameras

00:05:41.273 --> 00:05:43.440
that help make the physical digital.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.910
- When folks are actually
writing on this white board,

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.370
typically what you would be
doing is writing like this

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.580
and a camera would be behind
you shadowing other people

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.091
from seeing what it is that
you you're actually doing.

00:05:55.091 --> 00:05:55.924
- Right, they wouldn't be able to see

00:05:55.924 --> 00:05:57.090
actually what's going on.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.430
- This technology ghosts you out

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:02.070
so that people that are remote
can see what you're writing.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.060
- Can we test it?

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.923
- Let's do it.
- All right.

00:06:04.923 --> 00:06:09.330
(dry erase marker squeaking)

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.530
- [Adam Falk] I'm standing
in front of it now.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.550
- [Robert Norwood] See
what it's capturing?

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.230
- [Adam Falk] Yeah. I see what you mean.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.470
- So you have this new
space with all of these

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:20.100
new bits of technology and
different types of seating.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.620
Is it meant to encourage people

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.333
to come back into the office?

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.290
- I think it's meant to welcome people

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.100
if that's where they need to be.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.740
But if that person is
gonna do their best work

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.770
and have their best
contributions to their team

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.482
while being remote, that's okay.

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:40.315
(upbeat music)

00:06:40.315 --> 00:06:42.180
- [Adam Falk] That flexibility
may not be possible

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:43.380
for some companies.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.290
And others like Tesla
simply aren't interested.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:49.710
But LinkedIn sees Building
One as a kind of test.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.870
One that others could learn from.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.180
- To what extent is what we're seeing here

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.700
in LinkedIn's office the future of work?

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:00.480
- What we have is an awesome
opportunity to experiment

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.040
and to see what happens over time.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.560
And this is entirely flexible space.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.180
It might turn out that

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.220
what we need are more desks in the future

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.750
and that this could be changed into that.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:12.150
Or it might turn into a
completely different model.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.010
But the experimentation
and the observation

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.444
is really the key to
seeing what the future is.

00:07:16.444 --> 00:07:19.861
(upbeat music continues)

